course: dictionary
lesson: 8655
title: Seasickness: when your body feels muddled
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Seasickness is the sick, dizzy feeling some people get when a boat moves on the water. It can make you feel like you might vomit, and it can leave you tired and pale.
explain: Why does it happen? Your eyes and the balance organs in your inner ear disagree about how you are moving. Your eyes see a still cabin, but your ears feel the boat rocking, so your brain gets mixed messages.
explain: Use context to work it out: «Big waves are scary, but boats are built to handle rough ("choppy") water; seasickness makes fear feel worse.» Seasickness is a sick feeling in the body, not fear itself, and the sentence says it makes fear feel worse. Swap in your meaning to check it still makes sense.
fact: Seasickness means the sick, dizzy feeling you get when a boat moves on the water.
fact: Seasickness is a kind of motion sickness, caused by your eyes and balance organs disagreeing.
fact: «Choppy» means rough, with small, quick waves.
fact: People may take tablets, wear wristbands or eat ginger to help with seasickness.
fact: Sailors and explorers on long voyages often suffered from seasickness.
exercise: What is seasickness? | A sunburn you get on a beach | A cold caught from swimming in the sea | Fear of sharks | *A sick, dizzy feeling when a boat moves on the water
exercise: Why does seasickness happen? | You have swallowed sea water | Your stomach is too empty | *Your eyes and your balance organs disagree about how you are moving | The sun is too hot
exercise: In «Big waves are scary, but boats are built to handle rough water; seasickness makes fear feel worse», what does seasickness make feel worse? | *Fear | The boat | The seagulls | The fishing nets
test: Which of these is a way people try to help with seasickness? | Shouting at the waves | *Taking a tablet | Running down the deck | Swimming out to sea
test: What does «choppy» mean? | Calm and flat like a pond | Frozen solid | *Rough, with small quick waves | Very hot and dry
exam: Which sentence uses seasickness correctly? | Ella ate a seasickness for lunch | Ella ran to seasickness at the shop | *After the ferry ride, Ella felt pale and dizzy with seasickness | The seasickness was a bright blue pencil case
